From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 11:36:59 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: charge a pooled device's allocations to the
 host's pool

A host grants the GPU a share of the process's memory and has to keep every
allocation made for the guest within it. Only Venus devices were charged, and
only through the renderer: the OpenGL path, whose Zink device allocates every
buffer and texture of a guest through vkAllocateMemory, was counted by the
pool's usage but never refused, so a WebGL page could take the process past
the share.

Every byte either path makes is a heap that kk_alloc_bo creates, so the driver
is where one reservation covers both. A process can now state one pool for all
of its devices with kk_set_device_memory_pool(), exported by the driver library
beside the per-device structure that Venus chained. The devices the renderer
creates for a guest and the one Zink creates then share it without either
chaining anything, and a device that names a pool of its own charges that one.
Clearing the pool returns once no callback of it is running, so the host may
free what the callbacks name afterwards.

A pooled device reserves the size of every VkDeviceMemory heap before Metal
makes it and releases the reservation once Metal counts it, or has refused it.
A heap the pool cannot hold fails vkAllocateMemory with
VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DEVICE_MEMORY and allocates nothing, which Zink answers as it
answers any failed allocation, by dropping its cached buffers and trying once
more before it fails the resource. Memory a device imports names memory that
exists already and is not charged. The geometry heap charges itself, as
before, through the same pool; a Zink device now follows the pool-aware heap
rules instead of the fixed cap.

The device's other storage (command pools, descriptor pools, query pools,
events and upload buffers) is counted once Metal does and never refused: a
command buffer or pool that cannot get its storage ends the device's work, not
an allocation, and Zink does not survive it.

tests/test_device_memory_pool.c states a process pool and checks the limit, the
balance of reservations and releases, a refusal that charges nothing and leaves
the device usable, a pool cleared or stated with a callback missing, and a pool
a device names itself taking precedence. test_heap_growth.c made the guest's
allocation through the pool itself, as the renderer did; the device now charges
that allocation, so the test lets it and tells the guest's reservation from a
heap's by the thread.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
